\description{
A generic used across \{mathpsychr\} subsystems. Dispatches to a method for
the concrete subclass, which performs that subsystem's numerical work
and returns the results in whatever form is natural for it. See the
subclass-specific sections below for exact return shapes.
}
\details{
\strong{This is a descriptive device, not a model of the decision process.} It
takes the grand-average cascade curve, puts a sigmoid and/or a piecewise
linear curve with one hinge through it, and resamples recordings to see how
stable the resulting numbers are. It does not model trials, participants, or
the dependence between time bins, and the "p-value" in \code{diff_summary} is an
inverted interval rather than a test. Read the output as a structured look
at the shape of your data, a re-representation via lower dimensional curves
indicating where the curve turns, how steeply or whether groups differ
enough to be worth modelling properly. Do not consider its outputs as the
analysis itself!

Both curves are fitted with no box constraints. Fits are therefore free to
land anywhere, even at implausible values (e.g., an upper asymptote below
the lower one, a breakpoint outside the observed window).
\code{x$fits[[type]]$health} reports how often that happened across resamples.

Resampling is delegated to \code{{boot}}. The underlying object is returned
unfiltered on \code{x$boot} (all \code{n_boot} resamples; a fitter's columns are \code{NA}
on resamples it failed to converge on), and \code{x$spec} maps its columns to fit
type, group and parameter. Because the fitters are retained independently,
the resamples dropped from each are recorded separately on
\code{x$fits[[type]]$failed}, indexing the rows of \code{x$boot}. To inspect a resample
a fitter failed on, pull its recordings back out of the \code{{boot}} object with
\code{\link[boot:boot.array]{boot::boot.array()}}:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{arr <- boot::boot.array(x$boot, indices = TRUE)
failed <- x$fits$sigmoid$failed               # resamples sigmoid dropped
x$boot$data[arr[failed[1], ], , drop = FALSE] # recordings in the first dropped resample
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Each measure is routed by its AOI/Stimulus scope (see
\code{mathpsychr:::.measure_taxonomy}). Categorical Stimulus-scope measures
(\code{first}/\code{last_fixated_aoi}/\code{reference}; see
\code{mathpsychr:::.categorical_measure_stats}) are first converted to
per-identifier category proportions, at which point they are
computation-steps-wise identical to an AOI-scope measure, and distinguished
only by conceptually via \code{scope == "categorical"} for labelling:
\itemize{
\item \strong{AOI-scope / categorical}: compared across AOI/category. When
\code{grp_vars} is present (and \code{by_group = TRUE}), \code{grp_vars[1]} enters as a
second factor, with an AOI/category x group interaction test.
\item \strong{Stimulus-scope, continuous} (e.g. switches, watch time,
transitions; variables with one value per trial, not per AOI): compared
across \code{grp_vars} (up to the first two, in the order supplied).
}

Within either branch, every factor gets an ANOVA with pairwise/simple-effects
post-hoc. For factors with two levels, this is mathematically equivalent
to getting a t-test only, without breaking the workflow logic.

A per-level descriptive summary (participant-level mean/SD of the
magnitude for continuous statistics, or of the proportion for categorical)
is always produced, independent of whether a test could run for that
statistic.
}
